  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This book is showing an early but credible scholarly impact: OpenAlex records 6 citations, with uptake appearing steadily from 2020 through 2023. Its influence is concentrated in musicology and performance-related writing, while its open availability through multiple repository instances may be helping its reach.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Lucia D\u2019Errico\u2019s <em>Powers of Divergence: An Experimental Approach to Music Performance</em> is already being taken up as a reference point in discussions of music performance, interpretation, and artistic research. The citation record is still modest, but it is consistent: the book was cited every year from 2020 to 2023, indicating that interest has persisted rather than appearing as a one-off burst. The citing works are mainly articles and dissertations, which suggests the book is being used both in formal scholarship and in research training.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal is disciplinary relevance. Most citing works cluster in musicology and musical analysis, with additional links to music history, performance studies, aesthetics, and philosophical analysis. The venues and authors pointing to the book are internationally distributed, including Brazil, Spain, the United Kingdom, and Croatia. That pattern fits the book\u2019s argumentation, which draws on post-structural philosophy, semiotics, visual arts, and experimental approaches to written music performance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are also signs of broader methodological and policy-facing resonance. The record aligns the book with the SDG theme of reduced inequalities, and the funding metadata notes support associated with the European Research Council. While the evidence base here is limited, these signals are consistent with a work that engages questions of access, difference, and the conditions under which performance is read and valued.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Accessibility may be strengthening uptake: the book is openly available through multiple repository instances, and library holdings indicate subject placement in theory of music, musicology, and music performance. Public discourse data are unavailable in the archive, so the record does not support claims about media attention or event-based reach. Overall, the book\u2019s impact profile is best described as an emerging scholarly one, with its clearest footprint in research on musical performance, artistic research, and related theoretical debates.</p>",
